<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <meta name="csrf-token" content="<%= csrfToken %>">
  <title><%= title %> | JPSME</title>

  <%# The tab icon. settings.service picks it: an uploaded favicon, else the
      uploaded logo, else the bundled 16px-legible SVG. Always something —
      a browser requests /favicon.ico whether or not the page mentions one,
      which is what used to 404 on every page load. %>
  <link rel="icon" href="<%= locals.faviconUrl || '/img/favicon.svg' %>">
  <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="<%= locals.logoUrl || locals.faviconUrl || '/img/default-logo.svg' %>">

  <%# What Messenger, Facebook and Viber show when the address is pasted.
      Without these a shared link renders as a bare grey box, which reads as a
      dead link rather than as an organisation's site. %>
  <meta property="og:site_name" content="JPSME">
  <meta property="og:title" content="<%= title %> | JPSME">
  <meta property="og:description" content="Junior Philippine Society of Mechanical Engineers — connecting members, one event at a time.">
  <meta property="og:type" content="website">
  <% if (locals.ogImageUrl) { %>
    <meta property="og:image" content="<%= ogImageUrl %>">
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">
    <meta name="twitter:image" content="<%= ogImageUrl %>">
  <% } %>

  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/tailwind.css">
</head>
<%# Dark is the default for the public site. A page can ask for paper by
    passing pageTheme: 'light' — only the home page does today. %>
<body class="min-h-screen flex flex-col overflow-x-hidden <%= locals.pageTheme === 'light' ? 'bg-paper-50 text-pen-700' : 'bg-ink-900 text-white' %>">
  <%- include('partials/navbar') %>
  <main class="public-main flex-1 <%= title === 'Home' ? 'w-full px-4 py-8 sm:px-6 lg:px-8' : 'max-w-6xl w-full mx-auto px-4 py-12 sm:px-6 lg:px-8' %>">
    <%- body %>
  </main>
  <%- include('partials/footer') %>
  <script src="/js/api.js"></script>
  <%# Loads the built-in challenge image. Does nothing on a page with no
      challenge slot, and nothing at all once Turnstile keys are set, since the
      partial then renders Cloudflare's widget instead. %>
  <script src="/js/captcha.js"></script>
  <% if (locals.turnstileSiteKey) { %>
    <%# Cloudflare's own loader. Renders every .cf-turnstile on the page and
        writes each result into that widget's captchaToken input. Deferred so it
        never delays first paint of a form somebody is about to fill in. %>
    <script src="https://challenges.cloudflare.com/turnstile/v0/api.js" async defer></script>
  <% } %>
  <% if (locals.pageTheme !== 'light') { %>
  <style nonce="<%= cspNonce %>">
    /*
      This whole block is the DARK theme's remap, and it is skipped entirely on
      a light page. It used to run on every public page, which meant the light
      home page was having its card shadows stripped by the last rule here —
      cards on paper separate by border and shadow alone, so removing the shadow
      removed half of what made them read as cards.

      The public site is dark, but its views are written with light-theme
      utility classes that get remapped here. That means any class NOT listed
      below keeps its light value and renders dark-on-dark — which is exactly
      how the registration panel and the numbered section headings ended up
      invisible. The lists were covering 11 of the 34 text colours the public
      views actually use.

      So these are now organised by role and cover the whole shade range of
      each family, rather than the specific classes someone happened to write:

        700-950  strong text        -> #fff
        300-600  secondary text     -> #9a98b3
        accents  keep their hue, lightened to read on the dark ground

      Adding a new shade within these families now works without anyone having
      to remember to update this block. Opacity modifiers (text-slate-900/60)
      still escape it — Tailwind emits those as differently-named classes —
      so avoid them in public views.
    */

    /* Surfaces */
    .public-main .bg-white, .public-main .bg-gray-50, .public-main .bg-slate-50 { background-color:#25225d !important; }
    .public-main .bg-indigo-950 { background-color:#1f1c4f !important; }
    .public-main .bg-indigo-50, .public-main .bg-amber-50, .public-main .bg-gray-100,
    .public-main .bg-slate-100, .public-main .bg-green-50, .public-main .bg-red-50 { background-color:#1f1c4f !important; }

    /* Borders */
    .public-main .border-gray-100, .public-main .border-gray-200, .public-main .border-gray-300,
    .public-main .border-slate-100, .public-main .border-slate-200, .public-main .border-slate-300,
    .public-main .border-indigo-200 { border-color:#322d8b !important; }

    /* Strong text — headings and emphasised copy */
    .public-main .text-gray-800, .public-main .text-gray-900,
    .public-main .text-slate-800, .public-main .text-slate-900,
    .public-main .text-indigo-900, .public-main .text-indigo-950 { color:#fff !important; }

    /* Secondary text */
    .public-main .text-gray-300, .public-main .text-gray-400, .public-main .text-gray-500,
    .public-main .text-gray-600, .public-main .text-gray-700,
    .public-main .text-slate-300, .public-main .text-slate-400, .public-main .text-slate-500,
    .public-main .text-slate-600, .public-main .text-slate-700,
    .public-main .text-indigo-100, .public-main .text-indigo-200 { color:#9a98b3 !important; }

    /* Accents — hue preserved, lightened enough to read on the dark ground */
    .public-main .text-indigo-600, .public-main .text-indigo-700, .public-main .text-indigo-800,
    .public-main .text-amber-600, .public-main .text-amber-700, .public-main .text-amber-800,
    .public-main .text-amber-900 { color:#ecb827 !important; }
    .public-main .text-green-600, .public-main .text-green-700, .public-main .text-green-800,
    .public-main .text-emerald-600, .public-main .text-emerald-700 { color:#86c79a !important; }
    .public-main .text-red-600, .public-main .text-red-700, .public-main .text-red-800 { color:#d08a8a !important; }
    .public-main .text-sky-600, .public-main .text-sky-700 { color:#7fb4d6 !important; }

    /* Form controls */
    .public-main input, .public-main select, .public-main textarea { background:#131131 !important; border-color:#322d8b !important; color:#fff !important; }
    .public-main input::placeholder, .public-main textarea::placeholder { color:#8685a3 !important; }

    /* Hover surfaces.
       These escape every rule above: Tailwind emits `hover:bg-gray-50` as
       `.hover\:bg-gray-50:hover`, and a `.bg-gray-50` selector never matches
       that class name. So each one kept its real light value and flashed a
       near-white panel on a dark card the moment a pointer crossed it — on
       nine public views, including this page's own nav rows and every
       pagination control.

       One step lighter than the card rather than one step darker: a surface
       that recedes under the pointer reads as disabled. */
    .public-main .hover\:bg-white:hover,
    .public-main .hover\:bg-gray-50:hover, .public-main .hover\:bg-slate-50:hover,
    .public-main .hover\:bg-gray-100:hover, .public-main .hover\:bg-slate-100:hover,
    .public-main .hover\:bg-indigo-50:hover, .public-main .hover\:bg-indigo-100:hover,
    .public-main .hover\:bg-amber-50:hover, .public-main .hover\:bg-green-50:hover,
    .public-main .hover\:bg-red-50:hover { background-color:#2b2871 !important; }

    /* Shadows read as smudges on a dark ground */
    .public-main .shadow-sm, .public-main .shadow-lg, .public-main .shadow-md { box-shadow:none !important; }
  </style>
  <% } %>
</body>
</html>
